{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Raut2011-Fig6b-SiO2-Si-Ge-on-Si",
  "Source": "H. K. Raut, V. A. Ganesh, A. S. Nair, and S. Ramakrishna, \"Anti-reflective coatings: A critical, in-depth review,\" Energy Environ. Sci. 4, 3779-3804 (2011). doi:10.1039/c1ee01297e - Fig. 6(b) and caption, p. 3783 (labels SiO2 123 nm / Si 47.8 nm / Ge 36.75 nm / Si active medium; caption: Si (RI 3.76)|Ge (RI 4.897)|Si|SiO2(RI 1.455)|Air, lambda/4 at lambda0 = 720 nm, n_active medium = 3.76, nH = 4.897, nL = 1.455, nM = 3.76); text p. 3784, Sec. 6.2.3 (ref. 20, Macleod)",
  "Notes": "Simulated three-layer quarter-wave AR on a Si absorber with an extra Si layer between high-index Ge and low-index SiO2\nSpecification: Maximise transmissivity into Si in the far-red zone 720-750 nm (p. 3784).\nReported: Text p. 3784: 'the insertion of an extra layer of Si between the high index Ge and the low index SiO2 has maximum blue transmissivity of approximately 96% (at 480 nm)'. Fig. 6(b) curve has no readable axis numbers. Caution: a quick recomputation with the printed constant indices gives T = 0.9995 at 720 nm (admittance 0.955) but only about 0.69 at 480 nm, so the review's '96 % at 480 nm' may be a misreading of the original Macleod figure; the quarter-wave design itself peaks at 720 nm.\nNotes: Paper lists from the substrate: Si (active medium) | Ge | Si | SiO2 | Air; reversed here so layer 1 (SiO2) is next to air. All indices constant and lossless as in the simulation (Ge 4.897 and Si 3.76 are real-only here; real Ge and Si absorb at 720 nm). Printed thicknesses are rounded quarter waves at 720 nm (exact: SiO2 123.71, Si 47.87, Ge 36.76 nm). The merit row is inferred from the stated 720-750 nm analysis zone, not given by the paper.\nLayers from Fig. 6(b) and caption, p. 3783 (labels SiO2 123 nm / Si 47.8 nm / Ge 36.75 nm / Si active medium; caption: Si (RI 3.76)|Ge (RI 4.897)|Si|SiO2(RI 1.455)|Air, lambda/4 at lambda0 = 720 nm, n_active medium = 3.76, nH = 4.897, nL = 1.455, nM = 3.76); text p. 3784, Sec. 6.2.3 (ref. 20, Macleod); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), differs from the paper: ours T 69.1 % at 480 nm, 99.9 % at 720 nm; paper maximum blue transmissivity ~96 % at 480 nm (text); quarter waves at 720 nm. The review’s 96 % at 480 nm is not what this quarter-wave stack gives with its printed indices; it transmits 99.9 % at its 720 nm design wavelength, so the review probably misread the original figure.",
